<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Košice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kassa, Kaschau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Košice, in Hungarian Kassa, is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, in the east of the country. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> it was a leading royal free town and the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>, with the Gothic Cathedral of St Elizabeth. It was a centre of the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> risings of Thököly and Rákóczi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a> was reburied there in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosice.jpg" alt="Košice" width="320" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ville Miettinen · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cathedral_of_St._Elizabeth_in_Ko%C5%A1ice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Košice lies at an altitude of 255 metres on the river Hornád, at the eastern reaches of the Slovak Ore Mountains. It is about 20 kilometres from the Hungarian border, 80 kilometres from the Ukrainian border and about 400 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, and a chain of villages connects it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presov">Prešov</a>, 36 kilometres to the north. The first written mention, in 1230, calls it Villa Cassa. The name probably comes from a Slavic personal name, Koš, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form Cassovia becoming common in the 15th century; another theory derives it from the Old Slovak kosa, a clearing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> from the year 1000, and researchers hold that its population had a German majority until the mid-16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in Košice from the 16th century but were not allowed to settle permanently; they could enter for the town fair and had to leave by night, living mostly in nearby Rozunfaca, until the ban was lifted in 1840.</p><p>In 1604 Catholics seized the Lutheran church in the city. The Calvinist Stephen Bocskay, in a Protestant insurrection against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, then occupied Košice, where the future George I Rákóczi joined him as a commander. Giorgio Basta failed to recapture it for the Habsburgs. By the Treaty of Vienna in 1606 the rebels gave back the territory including Košice and won religious toleration for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> nobility. Bocskay died in Košice on 29 December 1606 and was buried there. In 1661 the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> described the inhabitants as Hungarians, Germans and Upper Hungarians; by 1850 Slovaks had a plurality of 46.5 percent.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> captured the town in January 1945, and it served as a temporary capital of the restored Czechoslovak Republic until the Soviet forces reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. The Košice Government Programme was declared there on 5 April 1945. Ethnic Germans of the area were expelled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Košice is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and the economic and cultural centre of the east, with about 230,000 inhabitants. It is the seat of the Slovak Constitutional Court and, since 1995, of the Archdiocese of Košice. Its preserved historical centre is the largest among Slovak towns and holds the Cathedral of St Elizabeth, Slovakia's largest church. The city is known as the first settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> granted its own coat of arms. It was European Capital of Culture in 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Košice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns were small by western standards and often German-speaking, with Buda, Pozsony (Bratislava), <b>Kassa</b> (Košice) and the mining towns of Upper Hungary the most important.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After a string of victories he captured <b>Kassa</b> and much of Upper Hungary, and in 1682 Sultan Mehmed IV recognised him as king of Upper Hungary in return for tribute; Thököly himself used the title of prince.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li><li><blockquote>In May and June 1919 the Hungarian Red Army of the Hungarian Soviet Republic pushed into Slovakia, recently claimed by Czechoslovakia, and took <b>Košice</b> and much of the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/slovak-soviet-republic">Slovak Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
